        "thinking": "I see now. The CF package uses a container executor for running adapter processes. The adapters themselves (Claude, OpenAI, etc.) are executed via subprocess in Node.js mode, or via CF Containers in CF mode.\n\nThe `GET /api/adapters` endpoint is about listing the available AI model adapters. In Node.js, these are registered in `adaptersByType` based on what packages are installed. In CF Workers, there's no `adaptersByType` map initialized this way.\n\nThe user's question is: \"At most make 'adapters' for certain things the plugins may try to do right? Storage, etc, we have the adapters for each within the cloudflare env right?\"\n\n\nSo the user is pointing out that in CF, storage and database adapters already exist (R2, Hyperdrive), and AI adapters would work if they make HTTP calls to external APIs. The problem is that adapters relying on local subprocesses won't work in CF Workers, but the current endpoint returns an empty list instead of showing which adapters are actually available.\n\nLooking through the adapter implementations, most of the local ones (Claude CLI, Codex, Cursor, etc.) spawn subprocesses and won't work in CF. But some like the HTTP gateway adapter and potentially others that use HTTP calls should be available. The endpoint needs to filter and return only the adapters that can actually run in the CF environment.\n\nFor plugins, the real constraint is that executing them requires spawning child processes, which CF doesn't support—even though we can now list them from the database and the storage layer is backed by R2 in CF.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
